<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Ballad · 1912 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">It's a Long Way to Tipperary</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A music hall song of an Irishman in London missing home. British soldiers sang it as they marched to the First World War.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jack Judge and Harry Williams (authorship disputed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Jack Judge and Harry Williams (authorship disputed)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English music hall; British and Empire soldiers of the First World War</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Stalybridge, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 31 January 1912; published October 1912</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Ballad</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Jack Judge and Harry Williams (Williams d. 1924), published 1912)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/its-a-long-way-to-tipperary">It's a Long Way to Tipperary</a>&quot; is an English music hall song, credited to Jack Judge and Harry Williams. Judge, whose parents were Irish and whose grandparents came from Tipperary, was a popular semi-professional music hall performer; Williams, severely disabled since a childhood fall, wrote verse and songs and played the piano and mandolin. The two met at the Malt Shovel in Oldbury, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a>, and wrote 32 music hall songs together, many at Williams's home, The Plough Inn in Balsall Common. Judge could not read or write music, and Williams taught him the tunes by ear. In January 1912, performing at the Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in Stalybridge, Judge accepted a five-shilling bet that he could compose and sing a new song by the next night, and on 31 January he sang &quot;Tipperary&quot; for the first time. It was written as a sentimental ballad for Irish people living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Bert Feldman published it in October 1912 as &quot;It's a Long, Long Way to Tipperary&quot; and promoted it as a march.</p><p>The authorship has long been disputed. After Williams's death in 1924 Judge claimed sole credit, saying that Williams's name had been added to repay a debt. Williams's family showed that the tune and most of the words already existed in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1909, &quot;It's a Long Way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connemara">Connemara</a>&quot;, by the two men, and in 2012 they campaigned to have Williams credited again; the family estate still receives royalties. In 1917 Alice Smyth Burton Jay sued the publishers Chappell &amp; Co., claiming the tune came from a song of hers for the 1909 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>–Yukon–Pacific Exposition; with Victor Herbert as expert adviser, the court dismissed the suit in 1920.</p><p>Florrie Forde sang the song in 1913 but dropped it from her act; it was nevertheless the hit of that year. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, British soldiers sang it on the march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a>, and the Daily Mail correspondent George Curnock reported on 18 August 1914 that he had seen the Connaught Rangers singing it through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> on 13 August. Newspapers printed the chorus, and the song became a patriotic favourite of civilians. John McCormack's recording of November 1914 spread it worldwide. Commentators held up its cheerful, unmilitary tone against the songs of the enemy armies, but as the war dragged on many soldiers came to dislike it: the veteran and anthologist F. T. Nettleingham wrote that it was &quot;never Tommy's song&quot;, and the gap between the civilians who called it the soldiers' anthem and the men at the front became part of its history. In 1916 survivors of HMS Tipperary, sunk at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jutland">Battle of Jutland</a>, were identified by their rescuers because they were singing it in their lifeboat, and in 1942 British prisoners trapped in the sinking Japanese ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> Maru are reported to have sung it.</p><p>The song remained the sound of that war in later memory. It is heard in Jean Renoir's La Grande Illusion (1937), Oh! What a Lovely War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> (1981) and Das Boot (1981), and it forms part of the regimental march of Princess Patricia's Canadian Light Infantry. Its tune was reused for labour songs, university fight songs and the hymns of football clubs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Signs in the town of Tipperary in Ireland greet visitors with a joke on its title.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lament of an Irish worker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> who misses his homeland. Unlike the popular songs of earlier wars, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, it calls for no glorious deeds and dwells instead on the longing for home.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Up to mighty London</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Came an Irishman one day.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All the streets were paved with gold</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So, everyone was gay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Singing songs of Piccadilly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Strand and Leicester Square,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Till Paddy got excited,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and he shouted to them there:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to go.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To the sweetest girl I know!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Goodbye, Piccadilly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Farewell, Leicester Square!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But my heart's right there.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 48 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Paddy wrote a letter</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To his Irish Molly-O,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Saying, &quot;Should you not receive it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Write and let me know!&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;If I make mistakes in spelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Molly, dear,&quot; said he,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Remember, it's the pen that's bad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Don't lay the blame on me!&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to go.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To the sweetest girl I know!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Goodbye, Piccadilly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Farewell, Leicester Square!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But my heart's right there.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Molly wrote a neat reply</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To Irish Paddy-O,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Saying &quot;Mike Maloney</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Wants to marry me, and so</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Leave the Strand and Piccadilly</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or you'll be to blame,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For love has fairly drove me silly:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hoping you're the same!&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to go.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To the sweetest girl I know!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Goodbye, Piccadilly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Farewell, Leicester Square!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's a long long way to Tipperary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But my heart's right there.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Jack Judge and Harry Williams (1912). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/It's_a_Long_Way_to_Tipperary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="akXVvCk5v0g" data-title="It's a Long Way to Tipperary: John McCormack's record of 1914"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">John McCormack's record of 1914</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel John McCormack - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=akXVvCk5v0g" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">John McCormack</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1914</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">It's the Wrong, Wrong Way to Tickle Mary (J. Will Callahan and Charles Brown)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1915</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">It's a Long Way Down to the Soup Line (Joe Hill)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1915</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fluminense Football Club's first anthem</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1915</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cornish translation by Henry Jenner, in the Daily Mirror</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1916</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Further It Is From Tipperary, The Closer It Is To Berlin (Billy Murray)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">answer song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Vanished Army (Kenneth J. Alford)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1918</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Grande Illusion, film (Jean Renoir)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1937</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">For Me and My Gal, film</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">It's a Long Way to Mukumbura</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The writing of Tipperary (Bill Caddick)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1977</span><span class="sg-chain-h">song about its writing</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Das Boot, film (Wolfgang Petersen)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gallipoli, film</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Every True Son (University of Missouri)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, fight song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mighty Oregon (University of Oregon)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, fight song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">It's a Long Way from Amphioxus (Philip H. Pope)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hymn of Club Atlético River Plate</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Regimental march of Princess Patricia's Canadian Light Infantry</span><span class="sg-chain-h">part of</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">It's A Long Way to Connemara (Harry Williams and Jack Judge)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1909</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier manuscript with the same tune and most of the words, according to Williams's family</span></li></ul></section>
